7. \(F_\sigma\) sets: Countable union of closed sets \(\bigcup F_n\)`,
        content: `
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 border-l-4 border-indigo-600 pl-3">1.1 Open Sets and Closed Sets in \(\mathbb{R}\)</h3>
            <p>
              A set \(O \subseteq \mathbb{R}\) is called <strong>open</strong> if every point \(x \in O\) is surrounded by some open interval completely contained inside \(O\):
              $$\exists r > 0 \quad \text{such that} \quad (x - r, x + r) \subseteq O$$
              <strong>Why intervals matter:</strong> Any bounded open interval \((a, b)\) is an open set because for any \(x \in (a, b)\), choosing \(r = \min\{x - a, b - x\} > 0\) ensures \((x - r, x + r) \subseteq (a, b)\).
            </p>
            <div class="bg-indigo-50/70 p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-indigo-100 text-xs md:text-sm">
              <strong class="text-indigo-900">Key Topology Proposition:</strong>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-1 space-y-1 text-slate-700">
                <li>The <strong>union of any collection</strong> of open sets is open: \(\bigcup_{k \in I} O_k\) is open.</li>
                <li>The <strong>intersection of any finite collection</strong> of open sets is open: \(\bigcap_{k=1}^n O_k\) is open.</li>
                <li><strong>Counterexample for infinite intersections:</strong> Consider \(O_n = \left(-\frac{1}{n}, \frac{1}{n}\right)\). Each \(O_n\) is open, but \(\bigcap_{n=1}^\infty O_n = \{0\}\), which is a closed singleton, <em>not</em> open!</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 border-l-4 border-indigo-600 pl-3 mt-6">1.2 Structure of Open Sets & Heine-Borel Theorem</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Decomposition Theorem:</strong> Every non-empty open subset of \(\mathbb{R}\) can be uniquely expressed as the <em>countable disjoint union</em> of open intervals:
              $$O = \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty (a_k, b_k) \quad \text{with } (a_j, b_j) \cap (a_k, b_k) = \emptyset \ (j \ne k)$$
              This fundamental decomposition is what allows us to define the "measure" (length) of any open set as simply the sum of lengths of its constituent open intervals!
            </p>
            <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 text-xs md:text-sm">
              <strong class="text-slate-900 font-semibold">The Heine-Borel Theorem:</strong>
              <p class="mt-1">
                If \(F\) is a closed and bounded set of real numbers (compact set), then <em>every open cover of \(F\) contains a finite subcover</em>.
                That is, if \(F \subseteq \bigcup_{i \in I} E_i\) where each \(E_i\) is open, there exists a finite subset \(\{i_1, i_2, \dots, i_n\}\) such that:
                $$F \subseteq E_{i_1} \cup E_{i_2} \cup \dots \cup E_{i_n}$$
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 border-l-4 border-indigo-600 pl-3 mt-6">1.3 \(\sigma\)-Algebras and Borel Sets</h3>
            <p>
              A family \(\mathcal{A}\) of subsets of \(X\) is called a <strong>\(\sigma\)-algebra</strong> (Borel field) if:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 text-slate-700">
              <li>\(\emptyset, X \in \mathcal{A}\)</li>
              <li>Complement closed: \(A \in \mathcal{A} \implies X \setminus A \in \mathcal{A}\)</li>
              <li>Countable union closed: \(A_1, A_2, \dots \in \mathcal{A} \implies \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty A_k \in \mathcal{A}\)</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              <strong>Borel Sets \(\mathcal{B}\):</strong> The smallest \(\sigma\)-algebra containing all open sets in \(\mathbb{R}\).
              Every open set, closed set, countable set (like \(\mathbb{Q}\)), and their countable operations belong to \(\mathcal{B}\).
            </p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-3">
              <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 p-3 rounded-lg text-xs">
                <strong class="text-amber-900 font-bold">\(G_\delta\) Sets:</strong>
                Countable intersection of open sets \(G = \bigcap_{n=1}^\infty O_n\).
                <br><em>Examples:</em> Open sets, closed intervals \([a, b] = \bigcap_{n=1}^\infty (a - 1/n, b + 1/n)\), the irrationals \(\mathbb{R} \setminus \mathbb{Q}\).
              </div>
              <div class="bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 p-3 rounded-lg text-xs">
                <strong class="text-emerald-900 font-bold">\(F_\sigma\) Sets:</strong>
                Countable union of closed sets \(F = \bigcup_{n=1}^\infty F_n\).
                <br><em>Examples:</em> Closed sets, open intervals \((a, b) = \bigcup_{n=1}^\infty [a + 1/n, b - 1/n]\), rationals \(\mathbb{Q}\) (countable union of singletons).
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        `
