        formulaBox: `1. Lebesgue Outer Measure:
   \(m^*(A) = \inf \left\{ \sum_{k=1}^\infty \ell(I_k) : A \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty I_k \right\}\)
2. Outer measure of an interval is its length: \(m^*(I) = \ell(I) = b - a\)
3. Countable Subadditivity: \(m^*\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^\infty E_k\right) \le \sum_{k=1}^\infty m^*(E_k)\)
4. Cantor Set: \(m^*(C) \le (2/3)^n \to 0\) as \(n \to \infty\), yet \(|C| = 2^{\aleph_0}\) (uncountable!)
5. Carathéodory Condition: \(E\) is measurable iff \(\forall A \subseteq \mathbb{R}\):
   \(m^*(A) = m^*(A \cap E) + m^*(A \cap E^c)\)
6. Vitali Set: Non-measurable set constructed via Axiom of Choice and \(x \sim y \iff x - y \in \mathbb{Q}\).`,
        content: `
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 border-l-4 border-indigo-600 pl-3">2.1 The Quest for Generalized Length & Outer Measure</h3>
            <p>
              We want an extended length function \(m\) on subsets of \(\mathbb{R}\) that satisfies:
              <strong>(1)</strong> Defined for every subset, <strong>(2)</strong> Equal to interval length \(m([a,b]) = b-a\), 
              <strong>(3)</strong> Countably additive on disjoint sets, and <strong>(4)</strong> Translation invariant \(m(E + y) = m(E)\).
              <br><span class="text-rose-600 font-semibold">Vitali's Shocking Discovery (1905):</span> Such a set function <em>cannot exist</em> on the entire power set \(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R})\)!
              We must either give up countable additivity or restrict the domain to a \(\sigma\)-algebra of "measurable sets".
            </p>
            <p>
              Lebesgue defined the <strong>Outer Measure</strong> \(m^*(A)\) by covering \(A\) with open intervals:
              $$m^*(A) = \inf \left\{ \sum_{k=1}^\infty \ell(I_k) : A \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty I_k \right\}$$
              <strong>Core Properties:</strong> \(m^*(\emptyset) = 0\), Monotonicity (\(A \subseteq B \implies m^*(A) \le m^*(B)\)), Translation Invariance (\(m^*(A+y) = m^*(A)\)), and <strong>Countable Subadditivity</strong>:
              $$m^*\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^\infty E_k\right) \le \sum_{k=1}^\infty m^*(E_k)$$
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 border-l-4 border-indigo-600 pl-3 mt-6">2.2 The Cantor Ternary Set</h3>
            <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 text-xs md:text-sm">
              <p>Start with \(C_0 = [0, 1]\). At step 1, remove middle-third \((1/3, 2/3)\). At step 2, remove middle-thirds of the remaining 2 intervals, leaving 4 intervals each of length \(1/9\). At step \(n\), there are \(2^n\) intervals each of length \(3^{-n}\):</p>
              $$m^*(C) \le m^*(C_n) = \sum_{k=1}^{2^n} \frac{1}{3^n} = \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^n \to{n \to \infty} 0$$
              <em>Surprise:</em> While its measure is 0, every number whose base-3 expansion contains only 0s and 2s belongs to \(C\). Hence \(C\) is <strong>uncountable</strong> (has the cardinality of the continuum)!
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 border-l-4 border-indigo-600 pl-3 mt-6">2.3 Carathéodory's Measurability Criterion</h3>
            <p>
              Because \(m^*\) only satisfies subadditivity, Carathéodory isolated those "well-behaved" sets \(E\) that split <em>every test set</em> \(A\) additively:
              $$m^*(A) = m^*(A \cap E) + m^*(A \cap E^c)$$
              (Since subadditivity always gives \(\le\), to prove measurability it suffices to check \(m^*(A) \ge m^*(A \cap E) + m^*(A \cap E^c)\)).
            </p>
            <div class="bg-indigo-50 p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-indigo-100 text-xs md:text-sm space-y-2">
              <strong class="text-indigo-900">Landmark Measurability Theorems:</strong>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-slate-700">
                <li>Every set of outer measure zero is measurable (null sets).</li>
